# THE SETUP PASS WRITES ONLY WHAT A LATER PASS READS (2026-10-10, the own build's stage 3).
#
# WHAT AMD'S CODE DOES. The first pass of every dispatch runs one thread for every pixel of the guides (2560x1440 or
# 3840x2160) and each thread stores six things: the pyramid counter (the same zero into the same one cell, from every
# thread), a zero into the game vector field's X and Y, a zero into optical flow's vector field's X and Y, and a zero
# into the disocclusion mask.
#
# WHAT IS READ OF THEM, BY THE SOURCE (tag v2.3.0):
#   - THE GAME VECTOR FIELD (X and Y): the game motion vector field pass takes the maximum of what is there and what it
#     has, at every pixel of the guides. The zero is its start value: KEPT, every pixel.
#   - OPTICAL FLOW'S VECTOR FIELD (X and Y): written by one pass alone, and only at positions inside optical flow's own
#     size, 480x270 at a 3840x2160 display (ffx_frameinterpolation_optical_flow_vector_field.h: "IsOnScreen(iSamplePos,
#     GetOpticalFlowSize2())" before the one update), and read by one function alone, which tests every position with
#     the same words before it loads (ffx_frameinterpolation_common.h, SampleOpticalFlowMotionVectorField; the
#     interpolation pass and the debug view call it). The images are as large as the guides; outside 480x270 nothing
#     ever reads them. THE ZERO IS KEPT INSIDE THAT SIZE AND LEFT OUT OUTSIDE IT: 1 pixel in 28 at Quality's guides,
#     1 in 64 at DLAA's.
#   - THE DISOCCLUSION MASK: the disocclusion mask pass stores every pixel the setup pass zeroes (the two are
#     dispatched with the same size: ffx_frameinterpolation.cpp 1184 and 1236), with no read of what was there, before
#     the one pass that reads it (the interpolation pass, 1239). That pass runs in every dispatch that is not a reset.
#     In a reset dispatch it does not run, and nothing but the debug view can then read the mask. THE ZERO IS KEPT IN A
#     RESET DISPATCH (so that even the debug view draws what AMD's draws) AND LEFT OUT IN EVERY OTHER.
#   - THE PYRAMID COUNTER: one cell. It is stored by the one thread of pixel (0, 0), beside the frame counter that
#     thread already keeps, and no longer by every thread: the same value in the same cell when the pass ends.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-10 (3840x2160 FP16, the two-image set, three rounds, spread 1 us or less; the same images on every
# hashed pair, 120 of 120 at both guide sizes): the setup pass 36 -> 29 us at Quality's guides, 79 -> 62 at DLAA's.
# LEFT OUT: the cut fi-setup-by-clears takes the same pass to 17 and 30 us and needs no edit of the shader.
what the setup pass (I1) zeroes optical flow's vector field only inside optical flow's size, the disocclusion mask only at a reset, and stores the counter from one thread
passes ffx_frameinterpolation_setup_pass
wave64 it edits one shader file that both the cs_6_2 and the cs_6_6 (wave64) variants are compiled from; it uses no wave operation; not run here on a wave64 device
